<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>
    <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Dark">
      <!--
        Surface hierarchy: window -> shell -> surface -> elevated -> interactive -> selected.
        Surfaces stay near-neutral dark navy and lift only slightly above the window, so colour in
        the product comes from icons, metrics, status and accents rather than from card fills.
      -->
      <!--
        The page's own wash, over the acrylic rather than instead of it. This was an opaque
        near-black navy painted by the content border, which covered the backdrop completely: the
        acrylic pane existed but nothing was ever seen through it. It is black with alpha now, so
        the desktop reads through the blur.

        The alpha is not decoration. A see-through background inherits whatever wallpaper the
        machine has, and body text cannot depend on that being dark, so this wash is the floor that
        keeps contrast survivable under a white one.
      -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutWindowBrush" Color="#73000000" />
      <!-- What the window paints instead of the acrylic when transparency is switched off. The
           value the shell used before the backdrop existed, so the opaque look is the old one. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutWindowOpaqueBrush" Color="#05080E" />
      <!-- The title bar stays solid. It carries the application name and the connection state, and
           content scrolls under it, so translucency there put moving text behind fixed text. The
           glass belongs to the band inside the content area, not to the chrome itself. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutHeaderBrush" Color="#0B111A" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSidebarBrush" Color="#A60B111A" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurface1Brush" Color="#0F1620" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurface2Brush" Color="#121A25" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurface3Brush" Color="#172029" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurfaceHoverBrush" Color="#B21B2531" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurfaceInteractiveBrush" Color="#A6131B26" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurfaceSelectedBrush" Color="#BF14202F" />
      <!--
        Glass surfaces. The alpha is low enough that what is behind genuinely shows through — at the
        near-opaque value this started with, the effect was invisible and the panel just looked like
        another card. The edge highlight is what sells it: a real pane catches light along its top.
      -->
      <!--
        Tone 1: the pane behind everything, deliberately the darkest thing in the window.

        Black rather than the near-black navy this used to be. With a tint of its own the backdrop
        was a colour, and the acrylic only ever blurred that colour: what was behind the window
        never came through, so the effect was a dark blue slab that happened to be built out of an
        acrylic brush. Black tints nothing, which is what lets the desktop behind the window read as
        the background — see MainWindow, where the opacities that hold it back were opened up.
      -->
      <Color x:Key="NutAcrylicTintColor">#000000</Color>
      <!-- Tone 2: glass surfaces, lifted well clear of tone 1 so their transparency is visible. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassSurfaceBrush" Color="#8C3A4A66" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassBorderBrush" Color="#40FFFFFF" />
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutGlassSheenBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#1FFFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.45" Color="#08FFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#00FFFFFF" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <!--
        The edge vignette down the two sides of the content area. A pane catches less light where it
        turns away from the viewer, and this is that, not a border: two gradients that start faint
        against the edge and are gone well before any content sits, so there is never a line to see.
        The colour is NutHeaderBrush, the same one the title bar and the footer are painted in, so
        the page dissolves into the bar it is running under instead of darkening against it. Change
        that brush and these have to follow: the effect only works while the two agree.

        Every stop carries that same RGB and varies only in alpha. A gradient whose last stop is
        transparent black interpolates towards black on the way there and dirties the middle of the
        falloff, which is the one place the effect has to be invisible.
      -->
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutContentVignetteTopBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#FF0B111A" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.35" Color="#A60B111A" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.65" Color="#4D0B111A" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#000B111A" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutContentVignetteBottomBrush" StartPoint="0%,100%" EndPoint="0%,0%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#FF0B111A" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.35" Color="#A60B111A" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.65" Color="#4D0B111A" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#000B111A" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <!--
        The same pane under the pointer, catching more light: a lighter, slightly less transparent
        tint and a brighter edge. Both stay solid colours because BrushTransition interpolates
        between solid brushes and switches outright between anything else — a gradient here would
        make the edge snap on, which is the one thing the effect must not do.

        The step is deliberately small. Glass that jumps to near-white under the pointer stops
        reading as a material and starts reading as a selection, which would compete with the
        accent bar that actually marks the selected row.
      -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassSurfaceHoverBrush" Color="#A6485A78" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassBorderHoverBrush" Color="#73FFFFFF" />
      <!--
        A row sits inside a pane, and the pointer is on both at once: hovering a navigation item
        also hovers the sidebar it lives in. So the row needs its own step above the pane's hover,
        or the two land on the same tint and the row vanishes into the surface exactly when it is
        being pointed at. Same family, one rung higher.
      -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassRowHoverBrush" Color="#B35F749B" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutBorderBrush" Color="#222C3B" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutBorderSubtleBrush" Color="#1A2230" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutBorderEmphasisBrush" Color="#2E3D50" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutTextPrimaryBrush" Color="#EDF2F8" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutTextSecondaryBrush" Color="#9AACC0" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutTextMutedBrush" Color="#687D95" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCardSmallTextBrush" Color="#B6C5D5" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSelectionBrush" Color="#14202F" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCodeSurfaceBrush" Color="#070B12" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutOverlayScrimBrush" Color="#A6040910" />
      <!-- Discreet depth: card sheen and selected-row glow used sparingly by shared styles. -->
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutCardSheenBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#111823" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#0E141D" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutElevatedSheenBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#141C27" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#101720" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutSelectedSheenBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="100%,0%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#CC16243A" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#99111A26" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <!-- Compatibility aliases keep legacy T24B page surfaces on the NutManager palette. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlBackgroundBaseLowBrush" Color="#0F1620" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlBackgroundChromeMediumLowBrush" Color="#172029" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseMediumBrush" Color="#9AACC0" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseMediumLowBrush" Color="#222C3B" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlHighlightListLowBrush" Color="#14202F" />
    </ResourceDictionary>
    <ResourceDictionary x:Key="Light">
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutWindowBrush" Color="#EEF2F8" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutWindowOpaqueBrush" Color="#EEF2F8" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutHeaderBrush" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSidebarBrush" Color="#A6F7F9FD" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurface1Brush" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurface2Brush" Color="#F7F9FD" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurface3Brush" Color="#EDF2F9" />
      <!-- Same two tones inverted: a tinted pane behind, near-white glass above it. -->
      <Color x:Key="NutAcrylicTintColor">#DDE4EF</Color>
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassSurfaceBrush" Color="#A6FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassBorderBrush" Color="#73FFFFFF" />
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutGlassSheenBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#B2FFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.45" Color="#3DFFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#00FFFFFF" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <!-- Light's header is white, so light's vignette fades to white. Same alpha ramp as dark:
           this is no longer a shadow whose weight has to be tuned per theme, it is the bar's own
           colour reaching a little way into the page. -->
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutContentVignetteTopBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.35" Color="#A6FFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.65" Color="#4DFFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#00FFFFFF" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutContentVignetteBottomBrush" StartPoint="0%,100%" EndPoint="0%,0%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#FFFFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.35" Color="#A6FFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="0.65" Color="#4DFFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#00FFFFFF" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <!--
        Light glass brightens towards opaque white, but its edge cannot: white on near-white is not
        an edge. The hover border takes the tinted emphasis colour instead, which is what the edge of
        a real pane does on a light ground — it refracts and reads darker, not lighter.
      -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassSurfaceHoverBrush" Color="#D9FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassBorderHoverBrush" Color="#B39DB6DA" />
      <!-- Light glass hovers at nearly opaque white, so the row above it cannot get lighter and
           takes a tint instead — the same blue the selected surfaces use, one step weaker. -->
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGlassRowHoverBrush" Color="#D9DCE9FA" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurfaceHoverBrush" Color="#E4ECF9" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurfaceInteractiveBrush" Color="#F2F6FC" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSurfaceSelectedBrush" Color="#E1EBFD" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutBorderBrush" Color="#C6D2E2" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutBorderSubtleBrush" Color="#DFE6F0" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutBorderEmphasisBrush" Color="#9DB6DA" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutTextPrimaryBrush" Color="#111C2B" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutTextSecondaryBrush" Color="#48586C" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutTextMutedBrush" Color="#6B7C93" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCardSmallTextBrush" Color="#48586C" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutSelectionBrush" Color="#E1EBFD" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCodeSurfaceBrush" Color="#F1F5FA" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutOverlayScrimBrush" Color="#59091426" />
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutCardSheenBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#FFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#F8FAFE" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutElevatedSheenBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="0%,100%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#FFFFFF" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#F2F6FC" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <LinearGradientBrush x:Key="NutSelectedSheenBrush" StartPoint="0%,0%" EndPoint="100%,0%">
        <GradientStop Offset="0" Color="#CCDCE9FD" />
        <GradientStop Offset="1" Color="#99EDF3FD" />
      </LinearGradientBrush>
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlBackgroundBaseLowBrush" Color="#FFFFFF" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlBackgroundChromeMediumLowBrush" Color="#EDF2F9" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseMediumBrush" Color="#48586C" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlForegroundBaseMediumLowBrush" Color="#C6D2E2" />
      <SolidColorBrush x:Key="SystemControlHighlightListLowBrush" Color="#E1EBFD" />
    </ResourceDictionary>
  </ResourceDictionary.ThemeDictionaries>

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutAccentBrush" Color="#3B82F6" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutAccentBrightBrush" Color="#60A5FA" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutOnAccentBrush" Color="#FFFFFF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutAccentSoftBrush" Color="#1F2F6FE0" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCyanBrush" Color="#38BDF8" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutHealthyBrush" Color="#4ADE80" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutHealthySoftBrush" Color="#1C22C55E" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutWarningBrush" Color="#FBBF24" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutWarningSoftBrush" Color="#1CF59E0B" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCriticalBrush" Color="#F87171" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCriticalSoftBrush" Color="#1CEF4444" />
  <!--
    Hover pairs for the semantic badges. The soft fills are alpha over whatever surface is behind
    them, so one definition reads correctly in both themes; the bright borders are the same hues
    lifted a step, which keeps a hovered badge unmistakably the same state it was.
  -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutAccentSoftHoverBrush" Color="#332F6FE0" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutHealthyBrightBrush" Color="#86EFAC" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutHealthySoftHoverBrush" Color="#3322C55E" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutWarningBrightBrush" Color="#FCD34D" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutWarningSoftHoverBrush" Color="#33F59E0B" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCriticalBrightBrush" Color="#FCA5A5" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutCriticalSoftHoverBrush" Color="#33EF4444" />

  <!--
    The status light's own green. It is a step brighter and more saturated than NutHealthyBrush so a
    small lit ball still reads as lit; the shared healthy token stays where it is because it also
    colours badge text and borders, where the same lift would look garish rather than luminous.
  -->
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutLedHealthyBrush" Color="#3BEF88" />

  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutPurpleBrush" Color="#A78BFA" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutFocusBrush" Color="#7DA9FF" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutUnavailableBrush" Color="#687D95" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutTrackBrush" Color="#2A3441" />
  <SolidColorBrush x:Key="NutGaugeTrackBrush" Color="#3A4757" />
</ResourceDictionary>
